        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.699,
            0.045,
            0.863,
            0.064
          ],
          "content": "A Story of Numbers"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.109,
            0.084,
            0.449,
            0.11
          ],
          "content": "Find the following products—"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.148,
            0.121,
            0.772,
            0.16
          ],
          "content": "(i) $\\cap \\times \\bigcap$ (ii) $9 \\times 8 ^ { b }$ (iii) $\\frac { \\pi ^ { b } } { \\Delta } \\times \\frac { \\pi ^ { b } } { \\Delta }$ (iv) $1 ^ { p } \\times \\sum g j$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.175,
            0.848,
            0.213
          ],
          "content": "Thus, the product of any two landmark numbers is another landmark number!"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.108,
            0.215,
            0.801,
            0.257
          ],
          "content": "Does this property hold true in the base-5 system that we created? Does this hold for any number system with a base?"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.817,
            0.211,
            0.905,
            0.264
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_19_image_6.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.108,
            0.262,
            0.8,
            0.303
          ],
          "content": "What can we conclude about the product of a number and (10), in the Egyptian system?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.208,
            0.32,
            0.236,
            0.341
          ],
          "content": "(i)"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.35,
            0.311,
            0.464,
            0.346
          ],
          "content": "9 9 \\times \\cap",
          "image_source": "images/page_19_equation_9.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.352,
            0.362,
            0.613,
            0.398
          ],
          "content": "?9 is same as 9+9"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.352,
            0.427,
            0.693,
            0.463
          ],
          "content": "\\infty , \\textcircled { 9 9 } \\times \\textcircled { 1 } = ( \\textcircled { 9 } + \\textcircled { 9 } ) \\times \\textcircled { 1 }",
          "image_source": "images/page_19_equation_11.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.235,
            0.485,
            0.77,
            0.507
          ],
          "content": "As these are numbers, the distributive law holds. So,"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.311,
            0.521,
            0.688,
            0.663
          ],
          "content": "( 9 + 9 ) \\times \\pi = 9 \\pi + 9 \\times \\pi",
          "image_source": "images/page_19_equation_13.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.202,
            0.677,
            0.347,
            0.697
          ],
          "content": "(ii) $9 7 1 1 \\times 0$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.245,
            0.696,
            0.607,
            0.734
          ],
          "content": "| is the same as $9 + n n + 1$ . Thus, $9 n \\cap \\times n = ( 9 + n \\cap + 1 ) \\times n$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.243,
            0.733,
            0.848,
            0.808
          ],
          "content": "Will the distributive property hold here? For the same reason that it holds for $( a   +   b )   \\times   n ,$ it also holds when one of the numbers has more than 2 terms. For example, $(a + b + c) \\times n =$ $a n + b n + c n . \\mathrm { S o } _ { 3 }$"
        },
